# the other pairs of legal_entity_code. The pair that DOES share a key space is the client
# hierarchy against the contracting relationship -- both legal_entity -> legal_entity over
# one authored roster -- and it was checked by nothing.
_shared_keyspace = (model.get("client_legal_entity_hierarchy").parents
                    == model.get("client_contracting_entity").parents
                    == ("legal_entity", "legal_entity"))
check("the client hierarchy and the contracting relationship share a key space, which is "
      "what makes an edge in both expressible at all",
      _shared_keyspace,
      "if these ever stop sharing a roster the overlap check below is aimed at nothing, "
      "which is how the first version of it came to be aimed at a pair that could not "
      "collide")
check("an edge present in both hierarchies is detected",
      _hy.overlapping_edges({("A", "B"), ("C", "D")}, {("A", "B")}) == {("A", "B")},
      "the one failure the whole separation exists to prevent")
check("disjoint hierarchies report no overlap",
      _hy.overlapping_edges({("A", "B")}, {("C", "D")}) == set(),
      "a check that fires on correct input is worse than no check")
# NO NON-VACUITY CHECK ON THE OVERLAP FIXTURE. One was written and removed: it asserted
# that a two-element set literal, written inside the condition itself, had two elements.
# That is a constant, not a check. The disjoint case above is non-vacuous because the
